WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: The Map That Wouldn't Sit Still

1
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:29.297
<v Old Timber>That song still gets me. Come close by the pond, little ones. The willow leaves are turning their pale backs to the wind, which is how leaves say, Something interesting is about to happen and I refuse to be blamed.</v>

2
00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:43.056
<v Old Timber>Today I want to ask you something. Have you ever wanted to know every step before you took the first one? Have you ever made such a careful plan that you could not hear a quiet bit of help right beside you?</v>

3
00:00:43.406 --> 00:00:55.692
<v Old Timber>God does not always hand us the whole map. Sometimes God gives a good next step, and watches us while we take it. Listen close. The answer is in the story.</v>

4
00:00:56.392 --> 00:01:16.854
<v Old Timber>It happened on a bright morning when the meadow grass was still wet at the roots, but the air above it was busy. Not stormy busy. Not scary busy. Just the kind of small, tickly wind that moves your hair, your plans, and one very important leaf if you happen to be a hummingbird with a clipboard.</v>

5
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:28.367
<v Old Timber>Zippy the hummingbird had found out, from a squirrel who had found out from a blue jay who had found out by having a purple beak, that the first blueberries were ripe beyond the bent birch.</v>

6
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:44.921
<v Zippy>Wait — wait — I just had SEVEN ideas! Idea one: map. Idea two: improved map. Idea three: spare backup map in case the improved map gets wobbly.</v>

7
00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:56.695
<v Old Timber>She had drawn the path on three oak leaves, two strips of bark, and the smooth side of a flat stone, because Zippy believed a plan became truer if it had enough copies.</v>

8
00:01:57.045 --> 00:02:08.051
<v Old Timber>Bongo the bear cub came thumping over with a berry basket on his head, which was not where berry baskets usually go, but he seemed very pleased with the breezy space.</v>

9
00:02:08.401 --> 00:02:13.817
<v Bongo>Friends! Blueberries need carrying. I got this!</v>

10
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:20.784
<v Old Timber>Shelly the tortoise looked at the basket. Then she looked at Bongo's ears sticking through the handle.</v>

11
00:02:21.134 --> 00:02:25.348
<v Shelly>Bongo. The basket is a hat now.</v>

12
00:02:25.698 --> 00:02:38.950
<v Old Timber>And Fern the fawn, who had been watching the bent birch sway at the far edge of the meadow, took one small step closer. It was the sort of small help God can tuck into an ordinary morning.</v>

13
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:48.242
<v Fern>Um... I noticed something. The birch bends toward the berry hill every time the wind comes through.</v>

14
00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:59.575
<v Zippy>Excellent! I will add wind-assisted birch-vector notation to Map Copy Four. Please nobody breathe near the important leaf.</v>

15
00:03:00.275 --> 00:03:15.931
<v Zippy>Okay — okay. Path meeting! Everyone stand according to optimal berry-retrieval geometry. Bongo gets basket support. Shelly gets patience department. Fern gets noticing committee.</v>

16
00:03:16.281 --> 00:03:21.618
<v Bongo>I have never been basket support before. I accept this promotion.</v>

17
00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:25.712
<v Shelly>Promotions usually come with less head wicker.</v>

18
00:03:26.062 --> 00:03:44.513
<v Zippy>Step one: follow the big red maple leaf to the left. Step two: turn at the pebble shaped like a potato. Step three: if the pebble is not a potato, kindly support the pebble and proceed.</v>

19
00:03:44.863 --> 00:03:53.466
<v Old Timber>The big red maple leaf lifted itself off the path, spun once in the wind, and settled on Bongo's nose.</v>

20
00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:57.717
<v Bongo>I found step one with my face.</v>

21
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:12.835
<v Zippy>Revision! Step one now follows the leaf-current event. Step two becomes step one-point-five. Step three is still pebble-related but much wobblier.</v>

22
00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.962
<v Fern>The birch is bending again.</v>

23
00:04:16.312 --> 00:04:24.680
<v Zippy>Noted! Adding birch-bend appendix. Appendix gets its own appendix if the wind has comments.</v>

24
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:32.927
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! A quest with paperwork! At last, grandeur has filing rules!</v>

25
00:04:33.277 --> 00:04:42.454
<v Old Timber>That was Garingo the parrot, arriving with a fern frond like a banner and looking ready to declare war on not-knowing, gently.</v>

26
00:04:42.804 --> 00:04:48.821
<v Garingo>I shall announce every turn so the blueberries may prepare themselves.</v>

27
00:04:49.171 --> 00:04:52.340
<v Shelly>Blueberries are not famous planners.</v>

28
00:04:52.690 --> 00:05:06.987
<v Old Timber>They set off. For a while, the path was cheerful. The basket bumped. The leaf-map flapped. Zippy flew from marker to marker, correcting the world with very tiny lines.</v>

29
00:05:07.337 --> 00:05:15.940
<v Zippy>Correction: the world is currently seventeen degrees off from my plan, but I am willing to ask it nicely.</v>

30
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:22.516
<v Bongo>Do we negotiate with the world by asking nicely or by bringing snacks?</v>

31
00:05:22.866 --> 00:05:25.252
<v Shelly>Usually snacks help.</v>

32
00:05:25.602 --> 00:05:31.723
<v Fern>I think the creek sounds louder on the right. The berry hill is past the creek, isn't it?</v>

33
00:05:32.073 --> 00:05:39.970
<v Zippy>Creek-sound received! I will file that under Helpful Sound Clue, Waiting for Leaf Proof.</v>

34
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.489
<v Old Timber>Then the wind arrived with opinions.</v>

35
00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:57.248
<v Old Timber>It lifted Map Copy One into a blackberry bush, Map Copy Two onto Shelly's shell, and Map Copy Three straight into Garingo's banner, which made him look like a very official sandwich.</v>

36
00:05:57.598 --> 00:06:02.510
<v Garingo>I accept this title. Official Sandwich of the Trip.</v>

37
00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:18.437
<v Zippy>Route review! We have Path A, Path B, Path B-prime, Path B-prime-but-with-slightly-less-emotional-birch, and Path C, which is mostly a feeling I had near a mushroom.</v>

38
00:06:18.787 --> 00:06:23.315
<v Bongo>I vote for the mushroom feeling. It sounds friendly.</v>

39
00:06:23.665 --> 00:06:27.069
<v Shelly>Feelings are poor trail markers.</v>

40
00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:31.450
<v Garingo>I shall question the mushroom with dignity.</v>

41
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.812
<v Fern>The mushroom is leaning away from us.</v>

42
00:06:35.162 --> 00:06:44.026
<v Zippy>That could be data. Or a mushroom boundary. Respecting mushroom boundaries is important, probably.</v>

43
00:06:44.376 --> 00:06:54.833
<v Old Timber>So Zippy made a fourth copy of the fourth copy, using a grass stem for a ruler and Bongo's basket hat for a desk. The desk sneezed.</v>

44
00:06:55.183 --> 00:06:58.666
<v Bongo>Sorry. My desk has pollen.</v>

45
00:06:59.016 --> 00:07:02.107
<v Shelly>Your desk is your head.</v>

46
00:07:02.457 --> 00:07:11.634
<v Old Timber>The wind nudged the new copy just enough to make Zippy draw a small arrow where no path had ever been, not even socially.</v>

47
00:07:11.984 --> 00:07:21.945
<v Zippy>Aha! Fresh arrow! Everyone, please admire how quickly my map has adapted to reality by inventing a reality.</v>

48
00:07:22.295 --> 00:07:25.207
<v Fern>I don't think wind-arrows count.</v>

49
00:07:25.907 --> 00:07:37.148
<v Old Timber>Now, there are two ways to be lost. One is to not know where you are. The other is to know exactly where your paper says you are while your feet are somewhere else entirely.</v>

50
00:07:37.498 --> 00:07:43.149
<v Zippy>Everyone pause! According to my map, the creek is on the left.</v>

51
00:07:43.499 --> 00:07:47.870
<v Bongo>The creek is making creek noises on the right.</v>

52
00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.468
<v Zippy>Then the creek has skipped the meeting.</v>

53
00:07:51.818 --> 00:07:55.954
<v Garingo>Shall I issue the creek a certificate of absence?</v>

54
00:07:56.304 --> 00:07:59.160
<v Shelly>The creek is present.</v>

55
00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:03.411
<v Bongo>It is very loudly present.</v>

56
00:08:03.761 --> 00:08:11.972
<v Zippy>Fine. New theory. The creek is on the right, the map is on the left, and I am between two difficult opinions.</v>

57
00:08:12.322 --> 00:08:24.373
<v Old Timber>She turned the leaf-map upside down. For one shining second, it agreed with the creek. Then Bongo turned his head, and the basket hat slid over Zippy's arrow.</v>

58
00:08:24.723 --> 00:08:28.937
<v Zippy>The hat has redacted my evidence.</v>

59
00:08:29.287 --> 00:08:34.389
<v Bongo>I don't know what redacted means, but my ears feel involved.</v>

60
00:08:34.739 --> 00:08:38.405
<v Shelly>Your ears are material witnesses.</v>

61
00:08:38.755 --> 00:08:41.611
<v Fern>Maybe we could stop and listen for a minute.</v>

62
00:08:41.961 --> 00:08:53.202
<v Zippy>We can listen while moving. I have invented moving-listening. It is like listening, but with reduced delay and increased shrub contact.</v>

63
00:08:53.552 --> 00:09:05.603
<v Old Timber>Zippy zipped ahead to prove it. She went past the bent birch, past the soft creek-sound, and into a little circle of young alders where every trunk looked like every other trunk.</v>

64
00:09:05.953 --> 00:09:14.974
<v Zippy>Minor update. I am not lost. I am, for a little while, surrounded by trees that look the same.</v>

65
00:09:15.324 --> 00:09:17.945
<v Bongo>We can see you, Zippy.</v>

66
00:09:18.295 --> 00:09:27.707
<v Zippy>Good. Excellent. Seeing me is useful. I will now prepare a report on how these similar trees feel.</v>

67
00:09:28.057 --> 00:09:38.985
<v Old Timber>Then Zippy stopped buzzing. Not all the way. A hummingbird is still a hummingbird. But her wings softened, and the paper leaf in her little feet trembled.</v>

68
00:09:39.335 --> 00:09:42.284
<v Zippy>I wanted the map to be right.</v>

69
00:09:42.634 --> 00:09:46.535
<v Zippy>I wanted to know before anyone had to wait for me.</v>

70
00:09:46.885 --> 00:09:53.189
<v Zippy>I heard Fern. I heard the creek. I just kept fixing the paper instead.</v>

71
00:09:53.539 --> 00:09:55.925
<v Fern>You were trying to help us.</v>

72
00:09:56.275 --> 00:10:00.327
<v Zippy>I think I was trying to make sure I would never need help.</v>

73
00:10:00.677 --> 00:10:10.246
<v Old Timber>The little alders clicked their leaves together. The creek kept speaking from the right. And Fern lifted her head toward the bent birch.</v>

74
00:10:10.596 --> 00:10:20.008
<v Fern>Maybe God is not asking us to hold the whole map. Maybe the next step is to listen to the creek and walk toward the birch.</v>

75
00:10:20.358 --> 00:10:31.050
<v Old Timber>Zippy looked at the leaf-map. Then she looked at Fern. Old Timber did not tell her what to do. He only asked one question from the shade of the willow.</v>

76
00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.301
<v Old Timber>Which sound is still steady when the papers are not?</v>

77
00:10:35.651 --> 00:10:42.895
<v Zippy>The creek. And Fern's voice. And maybe my own heart when I stop telling it to sprint.</v>

78
00:10:43.245 --> 00:10:46.989
<v Bongo>Also my basket hat is steady.</v>

79
00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:50.508
<v Shelly>It has accepted its calling.</v>

80
00:10:51.208 --> 00:11:03.651
<v Old Timber>So they did not make a new grand map. They listened for the creek. They walked toward the bent birch. They took one step, and then the next one, and then the next.</v>

81
00:11:04.001 --> 00:11:18.612
<v Zippy>Plan change: I will carry one tiny note. It says, listen first. It is the smallest map I have ever made, and I am suspicious of how useful it is.</v>

82
00:11:18.962 --> 00:11:27.687
<v Garingo>I shall announce it with restraint. Ahem. Friends, observe in a modest fashion: one step!</v>

83
00:11:28.037 --> 00:11:31.049
<v Shelly>That was medium-modest.</v>

84
00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:41.080
<v Bongo>I can do one step. I can do one step with snacks. I can do one step with snacks and a hat.</v>

85
00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:46.454
<v Fern>The creek is getting louder. And I smell blueberries.</v>

86
00:11:46.804 --> 00:11:59.168
<v Old Timber>And there, just beyond the bent birch, the berry hill opened bright and blue. Not because the map had finally behaved, but because the friends had finally listened to what was steady.</v>

87
00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:16.767
<v Old Timber>Blueberries hung in little dusty clusters, blue as twilight and round as marbles. The bushes were low enough for a tortoise, high enough for a bear cub, and exactly confusing enough for a hummingbird who had just promised not to make a twelve-page harvesting diagram.</v>

88
00:12:17.117 --> 00:12:24.463
<v Zippy>I really want to sort bushes by berry count, stem tilt, snack chance, and sparkle.</v>

89
00:12:24.813 --> 00:12:27.509
<v Fern>What is the next good step?</v>

90
00:12:27.859 --> 00:12:40.798
<v Zippy>Right. Next good step. Pick one berry that is blue. Do not audit the hill. Do not form a committee with the hill. One berry.</v>

91
00:12:41.148 --> 00:12:44.552
<v Bongo>I can be on the committee if the hill gets nervous.</v>

92
00:12:44.902 --> 00:12:48.150
<v Shelly>The hill seems composed.</v>

93
00:12:48.500 --> 00:13:03.424
<v Old Timber>Zippy hovered beside the first bush. She did not ask the wind for a final memo. She did not consult the mushroom. She listened to Fern's small question, watched the berry in front of her, and picked it.</v>

94
00:13:03.774 --> 00:13:06.395
<v Zippy>One berry completed.</v>

95
00:13:06.745 --> 00:13:13.049
<v Garingo>A modest triumph! I am whispering a parade inside myself.</v>

96
00:13:13.399 --> 00:13:26.416
<v Old Timber>And because one step has a way of making room for the next, one berry became two, and two became a handful, and the handful became enough for everybody to taste the hill without anybody needing to boss it.</v>

97
00:13:26.766 --> 00:13:34.010
<v Zippy>I would like the record to show that the blueberries are exactly where the creek and Fern said they were.</v>

98
00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.372
<v Fern>And where the bent birch kept pointing.</v>

99
00:13:37.722 --> 00:13:42.798
<v Zippy>Yes. I am formally saying sorry to the birch.</v>

100
00:13:43.148 --> 00:13:46.160
<v Bongo>I forgive the birch too.</v>

101
00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:49.366
<v Shelly>The birch is handling this well.</v>

102
00:13:49.716 --> 00:13:59.912
<v Garingo>I too shall handle this well. I shall not shout at the berry hill. I shall merely present it with my indoor grandeur.</v>

103
00:14:00.262 --> 00:14:04.476
<v Zippy>Indoor grandeur has a maximum volume, yes?</v>

104
00:14:04.826 --> 00:14:09.876
<v Garingo>Naturally. A tasteful tremble of majesty.</v>

105
00:14:10.226 --> 00:14:20.292
<v Old Timber>Garingo's tasteful tremble startled one blueberry off its stem. It rolled down the hill, bounced once, and stopped beside Shelly.</v>

106
00:14:20.642 --> 00:14:24.125
<v Shelly>The berry has accepted your announcement.</v>

107
00:14:24.475 --> 00:14:28.532
<v Bongo>Should I put it in the basket or salute it first?</v>

108
00:14:28.882 --> 00:14:35.734
<v Zippy>Basket first. Salute after berry count. I am growing.</v>

109
00:14:36.084 --> 00:14:44.530
<v Old Timber>They ate blueberries until Bongo's basket hat became a basket again, which he took as a step down but accepted with grace.</v>

110
00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:53.362
<v Old Timber>Later, back by the pond, Zippy set one blueberry on top of her leaf-map. It covered nearly the whole thing.</v>

111
00:14:53.712 --> 00:14:59.363
<v Zippy>It turns out a map can be helpful and still not be in charge.</v>

112
00:14:59.713 --> 00:15:09.299
<v Bongo>I thought helping meant carrying everything the right way. But today I mostly carried a basket the wrong way and still got to help.</v>

113
00:15:09.649 --> 00:15:12.505
<v Shelly>You carried it with commitment.</v>

114
00:15:12.855 --> 00:15:20.517
<v Garingo>And I learned that quiet grandeur may be announced softly, if possible before the berry rolls away.</v>

115
00:15:20.867 --> 00:15:28.486
<v Fern>And listening is not doing nothing. Sometimes it is how God helps you notice the next good step.</v>

116
00:15:28.836 --> 00:15:35.767
<v Zippy>God was not asking me to be the boss of every turn. God could guide us while we walked.</v>

117
00:15:36.117 --> 00:15:39.208
<v Old Timber>Mm. That is the heart of it.</v>

118
00:15:39.558 --> 00:15:52.654
<v Old Timber>There is a line in the Bible, in the book of Psalms, chapter thirty-two, verse eight. It says, I will instruct thee and teach thee in the way which thou shalt go: I will guide thee with mine eye.</v>

119
00:15:53.004 --> 00:16:10.828
<v Old Timber>That old word instruct means teach the way. Guide with mine eye means watched over, not left alone. For a child, that can be as small as stopping before you rush, asking God for help, and taking the next honest step.</v>

120
00:16:11.178 --> 00:16:20.199
<v Zippy>One step, then listen. I can do that. Probably. With a very small appendix.</v>

121
00:16:20.549 --> 00:16:24.528
<v Shelly>The appendix is allowed to be quiet.</v>

122
00:16:25.228 --> 00:16:36.652
<v Old Timber>And now it is time for Pruh-Fess-Or Hoot's Totally True Facts, the part of the show where Pruh-Fess-Or Hoot tells us a thing that is, as far as anyone can tell, completely true.</v>

123
00:16:37.002 --> 00:16:47.041
<v Professor Hoot>Professor Hoot here, with a fact that is completely true. Hummingbirds can hover in one place, and they can even fly backward for short moments.</v>

124
00:16:47.391 --> 00:17:05.633
<v Professor Hoot>They do it by moving their wings in a tiny figure-eight shape, which means the wings push air on both halves of the beat. Wing science means how air moves around a flying thing. There. A large idea placed neatly on a small shelf.</v>

125
00:17:05.983 --> 00:17:14.925
<v Old Timber>Thank you, Professor. A hummingbird can hover, yes. Though even a hovering hummingbird still has to choose where to go next.</v>

126
00:17:15.625 --> 00:17:23.757
<v Old Timber>And now it's time for Mudpuppy Minutes, the part of the show where Splish, Splash, and Splosh tell us a thing they have learned.</v>

127
00:17:24.107 --> 00:17:27.119
<v Splish>Maps are leaves that got ideas.</v>

128
00:17:27.469 --> 00:17:31.526
<v Splash>No, maps are puddles that dried flat.</v>

129
00:17:31.876 --> 00:17:36.482
<v Splosh>Incorrect. A map is a snack with bossy lines.</v>

130
00:17:36.832 --> 00:17:39.531
<v Splish>I followed a snack once.</v>

131
00:17:39.881 --> 00:17:42.084
<v Splash>Did it lead anywhere?</v>

132
00:17:42.434 --> 00:17:44.558
<v Splish>Into my mouth.</v>

133
00:17:44.908 --> 00:17:47.607
<v Splosh>Then the snack was accurate.</v>

134
00:17:47.957 --> 00:17:57.369
<v Old Timber>A map is a drawing that helps you know where things are and how to get there. Snacks may come along, but they are not in charge of geography.</v>

135
00:17:58.069 --> 00:18:08.526
<v Old Timber>So if your plan moves today, little one, you can stop and listen. Ask God for the next good step. Then take that step with the courage you have.</v>

136
00:18:08.876 --> 00:18:14.605
<v Old Timber>Until the next breeze brings us together, this is Old Timber at Shiloh Creek.</v>

137
00:18:14.955 --> 00:18:18.856
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>

138
00:18:19.206 --> 00:18:25.092
<v Old Timber>Mm. And if you see a basket wearing a bear, do wave kindly.</v>
